<%# See EnvironmentHelper for why this is staging-only and why it is chrome rather than content. %>
<% if environment_ribbon? %>
  <%# `fixed: false` for the signed-out layout, whose header is in normal flow: a fixed ribbon there
      would cover it, and a static one needs no offsets at all, so it also skips the controller. %>
  <% fixed = local_assigns.fetch(:fixed, true) %>
  <% position = fixed ? "fixed top-0 left-0 z-60" : "relative" %>
  <%# `min-h-8`, not `h-8`, and it grows **downward**.

      A rigid 32px box with centred content overflows both ways once the text is taller than the box,
      and the half that goes up is above the top of the viewport, where a fixed element cannot be
      scrolled to. Measured at 200% text on a 320px phone - which is WCAG 1.4.4, an AA requirement -
      the sentence needed 128px, and it started at y=-48. The first two lines were simply unreachable
      and the rest covered the header.

      Its height is published as `--sitf-ribbon-h` by `ribbon-offset`, and everything fixed below it
      is expressed against that; see chrome.css.

      z-60, above the z-50 drawers. Measured, not assumed: at z-50 it already stayed on top of an open
      mobile drawer, because it is later in the DOM. That is the tie design.md's stacking section calls
      fragile, and the remedy it gives is an explicit rank. %>
  <div class="<%= position %> flex min-h-8 w-full items-center justify-center gap-2 bg-amber-100 px-4 py-1 text-center text-xs leading-tight font-medium text-amber-900"
       <%# tag.attributes, not an interpolated string: an interpolated optional attribute renders
           **unquoted**, and neither CSS nor Capybara will match it. %>
       <%= tag.attributes(data: { controller: ("ribbon-offset" if fixed) }) %>
       role="status"
       data-testid="environment-ribbon">
    <%= lucide_icon "triangle-alert", class: "size-4 shrink-0", aria: { hidden: true } %>
    <%# Names the deployment first, then the one consequence that matters, in two short sentences.

        **No em dash.** A dash asks the reader to work out how the halves relate; a full stop just
        states them. It also made the line read as one long clause, which is part of why it wrapped.

        Leading with the environment's name is what the field ships: GitLab's "You are on a staging
        environment", Salesforce's "Sandbox", Shopify's "Development store". The second sentence is
        ours, because this product moves money into student portfolios.

        **"These are not real students", not "nothing here affects real students."** The second is a
        stronger promise than the deployment can keep - staging is configured with real SMTP, so an
        action here can still send mail. What is reliably true is that the records are fake, and that
        is also the thing worth knowing before pressing Finalize.

        Length is a constraint, not an afterthought: at 375px there are 304px for text, and the first
        draft measured 307px, so it wrapped by three pixels. This one is 219px. The ribbon grows to fit
        either way - see below - but a phone should not need two lines to say this. %>
    <span>Staging site. These are not real students.</span>
  </div>
<% end %>
